  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_nMdjX5sBD6pTyMTf",
   "name": "Temperature & energy of particles",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Temperature & energy of particles"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- As the **temperature **of a gas increases, so does the **average speed** of particles in the gas\n\n  - At higher temperatures, the particles have more **kinetic energy**\n- The amount of pressure that a gas exerts on its container is dependent on the temperature of the gas\n\n  - This is because particles gain **kinetic energy** as their temperature increases"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_KW43TWBBWmQxKDJn",
   "name": "Pressure & force of particles in a gas",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Pressure & force of particles in a gas"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Extended Tier Only"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Gases fill their container\n- The pressure is defined as the **force per unit area**"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$p=\\frac{F}{A}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $p$ = pressure in pascals Pa\n  - $F$ = force in newtons N\n  - $A$ = area in metres-squared m<sup>2</sup>\n- This equation and the particle model can be used to explain how particles exert pressure\n- As the gas particles move about randomly they collide with the walls of their containers\n- These collisions produce **force **at right angles to the wall of the gas container (or any surface)\n\n  - Pressure is force per unit area, so the force of these collisions exerts a **pressure**"
  },
  {
